- op: discuss  # 1a8d
  at: '2026-08-14T02:09:11Z'  # 1a8d
  by: agent  # 1a8d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1a8d
  clock: 5  # 1a8d
  nonce: 1a8d  # 1a8d
  message: "RAISED TO P1 AND CLAIMED AS THE DECIDED ROUTE TO INV-dabuf, recovered from the\npre-compaction transcript rather than re-derived.\n\nTHE OWNER ALREADY CHOSE THIS ROUTE, verbatim, on 08-13:\n  \"I'm not sure how excited I am about building mechanisms to figure out what\n   counts as 'production' for 100+ (or at least a couple dozen) language\n   ecosystems ... But what about making bash analyzable as an alternative?\"\nthen, concretely:\n  \"we have a catalog for python stdlib and other languages so why can't we do\n   an analogous thing for bash (or perhaps some conserved set from bash, tcsh,\n   zsh, etc), and why can't `>`/`>>` be part of that?\"\nand on the follow-up: \"yes I want you to do that\".\n\nSo INV-dabuf's fork is CLOSED, and it was closed by REJECTING BOTH filed\nbranches: not the packaging-scope axis (that is the per-ecosystem\n\"what counts as production\" machinery the owner declined), and not\naccept-and-disclose. This item is the third route the owner named.\n\nI REOPENED IT IN ERROR after a context compaction, presenting\n\"packaging-scope vs accept-and-disclose\" to the owner as a live fork. It was\nnot. Recording that here because the same compaction hazard will recur, and\nbecause the tracker -- not a session summary -- has to be what survives it.\n\nSTATUS UNCHANGED OTHERWISE: the retraction already on this item stands (a\ncatalogue CAN express redirection; the schema classifies non-call syntax via\n`attributes:`, and ADR-0016 never covered the shell's OWN writes -- only a\nLAUNCHED program's). Order stays REDIRECTION FIRST, taint-support SECOND.\nThe user-facing template question the owner raised is captured on this item\ntoo, with its mandatory disclaimer: a template covers WHICH launched commands\ndo what, never the shell's own redirections/pipes/builtins/env-var flow."  # 1a8d
