- op: discuss  # c9e8
  at: '2026-08-14T07:09:32Z'  # c9e8
  by: agent  # c9e8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c9e8
  clock: 7  # c9e8
  nonce: c9e8  # c9e8
  message: "SATISFIED — dev 6b041904cd. Re-run on the MERGED tree, both arms.\n\n  taint claim   rc 2 inconclusive + \"no taint catalogue (bash)\"  (was rc 0 confirmed)\n  boundary claim rc 1 violated                                    (was invisible: 0 edges)\n\nFixture: a script whose only statement is `echo \"$API_KEY\" > /etc/cron.d/pwned`.\nBefore today it produced ZERO edges — the same cron-dropper INV-larol was filed\nabout, written with a builtin and a redirect instead of `curl -o`.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. Analyzer emits an edge for `file_redirect`; the CATALOGUE\nclassifies the operator (io_primitives/bash.yaml), which is what lets a\ndivergent shell get different rows without touching analyzer code — the\nowner's conserved-set framing. `>` and `>>` are one primitive at two modes,\ntruncate/append on io_mode, so no INV-zumin row-order collision. A variable\ntarget (`> \"$OUT\"`) emits UNRESOLVED rather than skipping. Pipes deliberately\nout of scope.\n\nTHE OWNER CHOSE THIS ROUTE and the record is on INV-dabuf: both filed branches\n(packaging-scope axis, accept-and-disclose) were rejected in favour of \"what\nabout making bash analyzable as an alternative?\" then \"why can't > / >> be part\nof that?\" then \"yes I want you to do that\".\n\nA SECOND DEFECT SHIPPED WITH IT, BY NECESSITY. Adding a boundary catalogue\nsilently minted 3 bash taint SINKS (io_primitives is now the sole sink source\nsince taint_sinks/ was retired, 51e1d232f3). The census marked a language\nunsupported only when it had NEITHER sources NOR sinks, so bash — 0 sources, 3\nsinks — left the unsupported list and the blind gate was disarmed. Measured:\nconfirmed rc 0 on the dropper. Worse, the stderr note AND the\nunsupported_taint_languages field both went silent, so a DISCLOSED false\nconfirm became an undisclosed one. Predicate now requires BOTH ends, agreeing\nwith the propagation loop 70 lines down that was already guarded that way.\n\nThat is why the two halves could not be split: shipping redirection alone IS\nthe false confirm.\n\nTYPESCRIPT CHANGES TOO, disclosed in CHANGELOG and filed as INV-potuf: 6\nsources / 0 sinks, so its taint verdicts move confirmed -> inconclusive. That\nchannel is LIVE ON DEV and predates this work (control: remove bash.yaml and\nbash is 0/0 unsupported under either predicate, while typescript still flips).\n\nHOW IT WAS ADJUDICATED, because I got it wrong twice. A 3-judge panel plus two\nblind passes. I said \"must not ship\" — wrong; correct is \"must not ship without\nthe predicate fix in the same PR\", the defect being SINK-ONLY support, which is\nworse than mis-support because it looks analysable while guaranteeing zero\nfindings. I suspected my A/B was confounded between bash.py and bash.yaml — it\nwas not, and the refutation came by a route I had not seen (the failing test\nbuilds a synthetic map via --input, so the analyzer never runs). Two judges then\ndisagreed on collateral; two blind passes over all 89 taxonomy languages settled\nit — typescript and bash only, all 14 other catalogued languages carry both\nhalves, both flips toward FEWER confirms.\n\nFOUR OF MY OWN ERRORS, each green on unit tests, each caught by measurement:\nYAML used `name:` where the schema wants `functions:` (catalogue loaded 0\nprimitives); the analyzer first decided write-vs-read itself, defeating\noperator-in-data; the template declared `status: in_progress` (overlays MUST be\n`overlay` — it errors) and grouped commands under an invented `module: launch`\nmatching NOTHING silently; and the redirect branch read a `current_function` the\ncommand branch left behind, making attribution depend on tree-walk order (mypy\ncaught that one).\n\nA PARITY TEST NOW SKIPS FOR BASH AND THAT IS CORRECT, documented in its\ndocstring so nobody \"fixes\" it: bash declares no `subprocess` row because its\nopacity is PRODUCER-STAMPED (command_launch, INV-larol) and lives in\nPRODUCER_OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES, not the catalogue-declared OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES that\ntest enumerates. Adding such a row would be the mis-attribution ADR-0016\nforbids.\n\nSTILL OPEN — step two, and it must NOT be started casually: bash TAINT support\n(sources/sinks). The sequencing rule in this item's own statement is now\nsatisfied in the right order — redirection first — so taint support no longer\nmanufactures a false confirm on redirection writes. But it remains a separate\nchange with its own evidence bar."  # c9e8
